in Sheffield the strain of the social  care sector is all too apparent  this is one of many Care Homes counting  the cost of Staff shortages and soaring  bills  using agency staff is necessary but  expensive as energy and food costs are  rising along with interest rates it's a  time of apprehension about how  round-the-clock care can be provided  we've seen staff leave the system  through the pandemic we can't recruit  like we used to be able to recruit we're  not competitive with the pay rates and  they're doing a fantastic job with it  within you know our care facilities  within the trust but sadly we're not  competitive fighting their corner is the  Archbishop of Canterbury with a New  Year's Day message advocating for Urgent  action and greater funding our care  system is broken  but it doesn't have to be  we can rise to the challenge of fixing  it that means action from all of us you  me families communities government  caring goes to the heart of what it  means to be human it is hard Justin  Welby wants carers to be valued more and  paid more he's highlighted how less  demanding jobs can be more lucrative  adding to the Staffing shortfall  there were 1.79 million posts in adult  social care in England last year but  only 1.62 million were filled a decrease  of three percent on the previous year  leaving 165 000 rolls empty  and the number of vacancies is only  growing year on year  Boris Johnson promised to fix the crisis  in Social care after entering down the  street in 2019 but the implementation of  reforms has been delayed by political  and economic upheaval  British sunat government committed an  extra 7.5 billion pounds in November's  Autumn budget for adult social care over  the next two years  by the head of the national care  Association says that's not enough  there's been so much under investment in  Social care despite all the rhetoric  about how much money has been released  into social care the reality is it  doesn't get to the front line so our  providers don't see it and we can't then  use that to actually be paying people  more filling care jobs would ease some  of the burden on hospitals with the most  recent data showing around 14 000  patients were ready to be discharged  after treatment but couldn't due to the  lack of social care provision available  the population is aging and less healthy  adding to the burden on an NHS lacking  resilience underscoring why fixing  social care provision is seen as a  priority for 2023 with his latest  political intervention the Archbishop of  Canterbury has made clear what's needed  finding more funding for those receiving  and providing care Rob Harris Sky News
 

